\archtable

\subsection{Comparison of Full-Update Backbones}
\label{sec:backbones}
Table~\ref{tab:arch} compares the backbones when every node is updated at every step (stage-A weights). Graph-structured state helps: the plain graph GRU reaches event $F={{ggru_test2024_F}}$ and the best seg-adv-$F$ ({{ggru_test2024_seg_advF}}) on the 2024 set, ahead of the GRU, the TCN and memoryless pooling. On the chain graph, our AGN-Net cell is not better than the plain graph GRU; on the harmonic graph it reaches a similar event $F$ ({{agnh_test2024_F}}) but a lower seg-adv-$F$ ({{agnh_test2024_seg_advF}}). On the chain graph its mean event $F$ is {{agn_test2024_F}}, with a large variance across seeds: one seed is among the best single runs in the study, while the other two are among the weakest. Absolute scores are low for all models, mainly because of the annotation shift between training and test data. Offline detectors designed for this benchmark, such as EZhouNet~\cite{chu2026ezhounet}, report higher event-level scores. Our claims concern selective computation relative to a given model, not state-of-the-art detection accuracy.
